---
/**
 * The above-the-fold block on every page. It exists to own ONE thing that pages kept getting wrong: the reveal class.
 *
 * `.rev` is opacity-based, and Chromium excludes an element starting at `opacity: 0` from LCP entirely (CLAUDE.md §6). A hero built with `.rev` therefore hands LCP to whatever paints next, which on a heavy page is seconds later: /brand measured 4238ms LCP against 973ms FCP with the pixels themselves on screen by 1144ms. Six of the eleven page templates had it wrong before this component existed, each with its own hand-rolled wrapper class.
 *
 * The reveal sits on the WRAPPER, not on each child. That is safe here and only here: `.rev-load` is transform-only and never takes a stagger, so one transform on the container is visually identical to the same transform on each child. The "reveal the item, not the container" rule in §6 is about `.rev`, whose opacity fade would blank a tall block at once.
 *
 * Pages own their content and its order — the kicker, meta row and lead all differ, and contact puts its meta ABOVE the h1 while privacy puts it below. Pages also keep their own padding via `class`, exactly as `.card` works. What they no longer get to choose is whether the hero is LCP-eligible.
 *
 * `scripts/check/claims.mjs` fails the build if a page hand-rolls a hero with `.rev` above the fold, so bypassing this component is loud rather than silent.
 */
interface Props {
  /** Page-specific wrapper class, for padding and layout only. The reveal contract is not negotiable. */
  class?: string;
  /** `header` for a page head, `section` where the hero is also the page's only content region (contact, 404). */
  as?: 'header' | 'section';
}

const { class: className, as: Tag = 'header' } = Astro.props;
---

<Tag class:list={['page-hero', 'rev-load', className]}>
  <slot />
</Tag>

<style>
  /* Layout only. Size, color and the reveal all come from global.css and tokens.css, so this component adds no design decisions of its own. */
  .page-hero { display: block; }
</style>
